  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "q_caged-fretboard-ch3-checkpoint.what-it-adds",
      "kind": "choice",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "text": "You already had "
        },
        {
          "text": "1 3 5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " in all five windows. What does the major pentatonic add to them?"
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Two more notes per octave — a "
            },
            {
              "text": "2",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " and a "
            },
            {
              "text": "6",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " — making five notes in all: "
            },
            {
              "text": "1 2 3 5 6",
              "marks": ["code"]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Two more chord tones, so all five notes belong to the chord"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Five new shapes, one to sit alongside each of the forms you already know"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Nothing new — the same three notes, doubled onto more strings"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "answerId": "a",
      "explanation": [
        {
          "text": "The triad plus two: "
        },
        {
          "text": "1 2 3 5 6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", which in C is "
        },
        {
          "text": "C D E G A",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". No new shapes — the same five windows, two more notes lit in each, which is the promise this pathway has kept since chapter 1. The doubling in option D is real but it is what chapter 2 already described; the pentatonic genuinely adds pitches the chord does not contain, which is what option B misses. The full major scale has seven notes to the octave, and chapter 4 fills in the last two."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "q_caged-fretboard-ch3-checkpoint.which-are-chord-tones",
      "kind": "choice",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "text": "Of the five notes in the C major pentatonic, which ones is a "
        },
        {
          "text": "C",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " chord actually built from?"
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "1",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": ", "
            },
            {
              "text": "3",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " and "
            },
            {
              "text": "5",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " — three of the five"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "1",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " and "
            },
            {
              "text": "5",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " — two of the five"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "All five — that is what makes it the chord’s scale"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "2",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " and "
            },
            {
              "text": "6",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " — the two that were added"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "answerId": "a",
      "explanation": [
        {
          "text": "Three of the five: "
        },
        {
          "text": "1",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "text": "3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and "
        },
        {
          "text": "5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " — the "
        },
        {
          "text": "C",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "text": "E",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and "
        },
        {
          "text": "G",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " chapter 2 spent a whole chapter on. The other two, the "
        },
        {
          "text": "2",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and the "
        },
        {
          "text": "6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", belong to the scale but not to the chord. That split is the whole point of this chapter: the notes that are in the chord are the ones a phrase can land on and sound finished, and the two that are not are the ones that keep a line moving. A player who knows which dot is which lands on purpose; a player who only knows the box runs it up and down."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "q_caged-fretboard-ch3-checkpoint.caged-not-boxes",
      "kind": "choice",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "text": "You open the scale visualizer on C major pentatonic and switch the position toggle to "
        },
        {
          "text": "Boxes",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". The shapes on screen do not match the windows this chapter drew. What has happened?"
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Boxes",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " is a different five-window tiling with its own numbering. Switch the toggle to "
            },
            {
              "text": "CAGED",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " to get these windows."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "They are the same five windows, just renumbered from the nut — same shapes, different labels"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The visualizer has switched to the minor pentatonic"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The "
            },
            {
              "text": "Boxes",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " toggle only works for seven-note scales"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "answerId": "a",
      "explanation": [
        {
          "text": "Two genuinely different ways of carving the same notes into positions. The "
        },
        {
          "text": "CAGED",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " toggle gives exactly this chapter’s five windows, with these same form names. "
        },
        {
          "text": "Boxes",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is its own tiling with its own numbering, and its windows do not line up with the forms — so the shapes really are different, not just relabelled. The notes on the neck have not changed; only the boxes drawn around them have. Leave the toggle on "
        },
        {
          "text": "CAGED",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " while you are working through this chapter."
        }
      ]
    },
