    {
      "id": "q_caged-fretboard-ch3-checkpoint.relative-minor",
      "kind": "choice",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "text": "The G form’s twelve pentatonic dots are, position for position, the box most guitarists already know as the first shape of the "
        },
        {
          "text": "minor",
          "marks": ["bold"]
        },
        {
          "text": " pentatonic. What decides whether those same twelve positions sound major or minor?"
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The note you keep coming back to — land on "
            },
            {
              "text": "C",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " and it sounds major, land on "
            },
            {
              "text": "A",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " and it sounds minor"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "One of the five notes moves down a fret"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The major version sits at a different fret on the neck"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The order you play the notes in — ascending is major, descending is minor"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "answerId": "a",
      "explanation": [
        {
          "text": "C major pentatonic is "
        },
        {
          "text": "C D E G A",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". A minor pentatonic is "
        },
        {
          "text": "A C D E G",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". The same five notes, written from a different starting point — there is only one set of pitches under your hand, and nothing about the shape, the fingering or the frets changes at all. What changes is where a phrase stops. The G form makes this visible: it carries three "
        },
        {
          "text": "C",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "s and three "
        },
        {
          "text": "A",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "s, and the lowest note in the whole window is "
        },
        {
          "text": "6·5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", an "
        },
        {
          "text": "A",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " rather than the root. Anyone who learned this box by ear learned to resolve to its lowest note, which is why it has always sounded minor to them."
        }
      ]
    },
